# Orchestrate Skill Set — Key Points

3-tier autonomous orchestration. User hands mission to L1. Everything below runs headless. User commits at end.

## Skills

- `slay-orchestrate-l1-lead` — L1, mission lead
- `slay-orchestrate-l2-exec` — L2, executor
- `slay-orchestrate-l3-impl` — L3, implementor

Replaces existing `slay-orchestrate` skill.

## Topology

```
User
 └─ L1 (slay-orchestrate-l1-lead) — root slay task, user's PTY
     └─ L2 (slay-orchestrate-l2-exec) — sub-task, own PTY
         ├─ L3 (slay-orchestrate-l3-impl) — sub-sub-task, own PTY
         ├─ L3 (slay-orchestrate-l3-impl) — sub-sub-task, own PTY
         └─ L3 (slay-orchestrate-l3-impl) — sub-sub-task, own PTY
```

Hierarchy: L1 → L2 → L3. Strict. No skipping.

## Communication layers

Two distinct channels. Do not conflate.

| Layer | Mechanism | Direction | Purpose |
|-------|-----------|-----------|---------|
| **Inter-agent comm** | PTY (full terminal) | parent ↔ child | Authoritative: spawns, instructions, escalations, polls, replies, mode control |
| **Progress reporting** | `slay task` CLI | agent → own task | Observability: continuous log of what agent is doing |

PTY channel is how agents TALK. Progress layer is how agents BROADCAST state. Parents can read child progress via CLI (cheap) before deciding to PTY-intervene.

### PTY = full terminal

Child's PTY is a real terminal running an AI CLI (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, etc). Parent has **everything a human user has**. Not limited to text msgs.

**Commands.**
- **Text submit** — `slay pty submit <child-session-id> "<text>"` (handles multi-line, waits for attention state)
- **Raw keys / escape seqs** — `slay pty write <child-session-id> $'\e[Z'` (Shift+Tab), etc.
- **Read output** — `slay pty buffer <child-session-id>` (snapshot)
- **Wait for ready** — `slay pty wait <child-session-id> --state attention`

**When to use PTY beyond plain text.**
- **Shift+Tab** — cycle child's mode (plan / auto / default). Use if child stuck in wrong mode
- **Slash commands** (agent-specific, e.g. Claude Code) — `/compact` context when full, `/clear` to reset, `/effort high` to escalate reasoning, `/plan` to force plan mode, `/model` to switch, etc.
- **Interrupt** — ESC or Ctrl+C if child hallucinating loop

Parents (L1, L2) should treat PTY as full human interface. If a human could do it, the agent should consider it. Children respond to whatever they'd respond to with a human user.

